 <p class="section-label">A LITTLE BIT OF EVERYWHERE</p><h2>What to eat while you’re here</h2>
 <p class="fd-lede">Mauritian food mixes Indian, Creole, Chinese and French cooking, and the best of it is sold from street stalls for a few rupees. Here’s what we’d love you to try.</p>
-<p class="fd-script">come hungry, leave happy</p>
-<div class="dish-grid"><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/dholl_puri.jpeg" alt="Dholl puri" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Dholl puri</h3><p>The island’s favourite snack: a soft flatbread made with ground split peas, wrapped around bean curry, chutney and pickles.</p><p class="dish-w">Street stalls &amp; markets</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/roti_chaud.jpeg" alt="Roti chaud" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Roti chaud</h3><p>A thin, warm roti rolled around butter bean curry, rougaille or vindaye.</p><p class="dish-w">Street stalls</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/gateau_piment.jpeg" alt="Gâteaux piment" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Gâteaux piment</h3><p>Little crispy chilli fritters made from split peas. Dip them in coriander chutney.</p><p class="dish-w">Everywhere</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/boulette.jpeg" alt="Boulettes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Boulettes</h3><p>Steamed dumplings of fish, chayote or taro, served in broth with chilli paste.</p><p class="dish-w">China Town, Port Louis</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/mine_frite.jpeg" alt="Mine frite" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Mine frite</h3><p>Mauritian-Chinese fried noodles with egg, chilli and soy.</p><p class="dish-w">Street stalls &amp; restaurants</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/bol_renverse.jpeg" alt="Bol renversé" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Bol renversé</h3><p>The “upside-down bowl”: rice topped with a stir-fry and a fried egg, turned out onto the plate.</p><p class="dish-w">Mauritian-Chinese restaurants</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><svg viewBox="0 0 300 200" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="300" height="200" fill="#e9a23b"/><g opacity=".18" stroke="#fffdf7" stroke-width="10"><path d="M0 40 H300 M0 100 H300 M0 160 H300 M40 0 V200 M120 0 V200 M200 0 V200 M280 0 V200"/></g><circle cx="150" cy="110" r="74" fill="#000" opacity=".08"/><circle cx="150" cy="104" r="74" fill="#fffdf7"/><circle cx="150" cy="104" r="62" fill="none" stroke="#e8e1cf" stroke-width="2"/><g fill="#ecd29b"><path d="M96 104 a54 40 0 1 0 108 0 a54 40 0 1 0 -108 0"/></g><g fill="none" stroke="#c99a4a" stroke-width="3"><path d="M110 98 q40 -24 80 0"/><path d="M106 112 q44 -20 88 0"/></g><g fill="#d8a04a" opacity=".7"><circle cx="128" cy="92" r="5"/><circle cx="176" cy="116" r="6"/><circle cx="150" cy="122" r="4"/></g></svg></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Farata</h3><p>The Mauritian paratha, flaky and buttery, eaten with curry or pickled vegetables.</p><p class="dish-w">Markets &amp; roadside stalls</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/biryani.jpeg" alt="Biryani" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Biryani</h3><p>Layers of spiced rice with chicken, lamb or fish, and potatoes.</p><p class="dish-w">Restaurants &amp; markets</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/cari_rougaille.jpeg" alt="Cari &amp; rougaille" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Cari &amp; rougaille</h3><p>Mauritian curry, and rougaille, a Creole tomato sauce with garlic, ginger, thyme and chilli.</p><p class="dish-w">Most restaurants</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/fish_vindaye.jpeg" alt="Fish vindaye" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Fish vindaye</h3><p>Fried fish pickled with mustard seed, turmeric and garlic. Tangy and very moreish.</p><p class="dish-w">Creole restaurants</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/pineapple_w_chili_salt.jpeg" alt="Pineapple with chilli salt" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Pineapple with chilli salt</h3><p>Sweet Victoria pineapple on a stick, dusted with chilli and salt. Sounds odd, tastes amazing.</p><p class="dish-w">Beach vendors</p></div></article><article class="dish"><figure><img src="assets/images/food/alouda.jpeg" alt="Alouda" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="dish-b"><h3>Alouda</h3><p>A chilled milky drink with basil seeds, agar jelly and syrup. Perfect on a hot afternoon.</p><p class="dish-w">Port Louis Central Market</p></div></article></div>
-<div class="fd-block"><p class="section-label">TO DRINK</p><h2>Something to wash it down</h2><div class="drink-grid"><div class="drink"><img src="assets/images/drinks/phoenix_beer.jpeg" alt="Phoenix beer" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><div><b>Phoenix beer</b><p>The local lager, brewed since 1963.</p></div></div><div class="drink"><img src="assets/images/drinks/mauritian_rum.jpeg" alt="Mauritian rum" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><div><b>Mauritian rum</b><p>Try a Ti’ Punch, or rhum arrangé steeped with fruit and spices.</p></div></div><div class="drink"><img src="assets/images/drinks/vanilla_tea.jpeg" alt="Vanilla tea" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><div><b>Vanilla tea</b><p>Mauritian tea, often flavoured with vanilla. Taste it at Bois Chéri.</p></div></div><div class="drink"><img src="assets/images/drinks/fresh_coconut.jpeg" alt="Fresh coconut" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><div><b>Fresh coconut</b><p>Sold green and chilled from roadside stands.</p></div></div></div></div>
-<div class="fd-block"><p class="section-label">OUR SHORTLIST</p><h2>Where to eat</h2><div class="mo-grid"><div class="wh-col"><p class="mo-h">STREET FOOD</p><div class="wh-i"><b>Dewa &amp; Sons, Rose Hill</b><p>A famous dholl puri stop. Takeaway, cheap and always busy. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Dewa+and+Sons+Rose+Hill" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div><div class="wh-i"><b>Port Louis Central Market</b><p>Dholl puri, alouda and boulettes in one place. Go in the morning. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Port+Louis+Central+Market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div><div class="wh-i"><b>Flic en Flac beach stalls</b><p>Noodles and rotis by the sand, right where you’re staying. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Flic+en+Flac+Public+Beach" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div></div><div class="wh-col"><p class="mo-h">SIT-DOWN LUNCHES</p><div class="wh-i"><b>First Restaurant, China Town</b><p>Busy Cantonese spot with lots of dim sum. Book on Sundays. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=First+Restaurant+China+Town+Port+Louis" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div><div class="wh-i"><b>Escale Créole, Moka</b><p>Home-style Creole cooking in a garden, with homemade infused rums. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Escale+Creole+Moka" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div><div class="wh-i"><b>Maison Eureka, Moka</b><p>Lunch on the veranda of a 180-year-old plantation house. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Maison+Eureka+Moka" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div></div><div class="wh-col"><p class="mo-h">WITH A VIEW</p><div class="wh-i"><b>La Table du Château</b><p>At Château de Labourdonnais. Try the vanilla crème brûlée. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=La+Table+du+Chateau+Labourdonnais" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div><div class="wh-i"><b>L’Alchimiste, Chamarel</b><p>At the Rhumerie de Chamarel, with rum-laced desserts. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=L+Alchimiste+Rhumerie+de+Chamarel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div><div class="wh-i"><b>Le Chamarel Restaurant</b><p>Lunch looking out to Le Morne and the sea. <a class="mp" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Le+Chamarel+Restaurant" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Map&nbsp;↗</a></p></div></div></div></div>
